ring needs of  those here who are interested in running  and developing iron.i MDS for  regenerative medicine and cell therapy  but again as I've mentioned a few times  now we have some pretty obvious  limitations and challenges before us we  have a very limited amount of space that  we have to work with and a very limited  staff that we have to perform all of the  development activities and manufacturing  activities so I want to give you an idea  of what our manual manufacturing  protocols were looking like we use the  cell processing workstation as you saw  in the images earlier if you're not  familiar the still processing  workstation is a standalone isolator  type of system it is a class 100  environment on the inside once it's been  decontaminated it has its own  incorporated hydrogen peroxide  decontamination system it also has  everything else that you need to do cell  culture like a centrifuge a microscope  and an incubator the incubators are  modular so you can add additional  incubators if you need to but we don't  have the space for that so we just have  one modular incubator that's only 1.4  cubic feet on the inside contrast that  with a standard cell culture incubator  that has about 6 cubic feet of space on  the inside so we are working with a much  less space than what we're used to  having to handle all of our incubation  means we use multi layer flasks and with  all the options out there on the market  we were able to narrow it down to two  different types that we could use with  this modular incubator that those were  the millah for high flash which have  about a thousand square centimeters of  growth area and the corning hyper class  which RT 1720s  this is what they look like and for all  of our manufacturing methods we use  alpha mem and 5% platelet lysate we've  gotten away from SPS some time ago and  this is how we would do it we would  start by expanding MSC's up to passage 1  to create a master cell bank of vials of  MSC's  to do that we were able to scale up to a  maximum of 10 t1 thousands last that's  the maximum number of flats that we can  fit inside the modular incubator in  those 10 C 1000 last we could produce  enough cells about 200 million MSC s  to freeze down about 100 vials of 2  million MSC s per vial for our master  solving after that when we're ready to  create a batch of product we could pull  vials from the master cell bank and  further expand them up to passage 3 to  create batches of stem cell products  that are intended to be used for therapy  to do that we could scale up to a  maximum of 10 T 1720 flasks and at the  end of all this we could approximately  create about 4 doses of product and each  each dose would have a hundred million  MF fees in the bag from start to finish  which is from the raw bone marrow  aspirate all the way to creating the  very first batch of stem cell product  doses that manufacturing campaign would  take anywhere from six to eight weeks to  complete so there are some pretty  obvious limitations to doing it this way  first of all you can only fit a maximum  of ten of either type of multi-layer  flasks inside the incubator so you're  really limited in how many cells you can  produce the whole process from start to  finish is extremely tedious very  time-consuming and labor-intensive you  really absolutely need at least two  technologists to perform these tasks  efficiently I have done it with one  before during the development phase and  it just is not feasible you consume a  huge number or huge volume of culture  medium doing it this way each of the  Corning hyper flasks requires five  hundred and sixty-five milliliters of  culture medium to fill up the flask  completely which you have to do with  those flasks and so you can imagine  towards the end when you have ten hyper  flasks and every time you do a medium  change you need six liters approximately  six liters of media so you can see how  the cost and medium and play of lysate  can really add up doing it  swing and it's a relatively long period  of time from start to finish and at the  end you really do not get that many  doses of products so you're putting in a  lot and you're not really getting a lot  out of it so it didn't take long for us  to realize that we needed to make some  changes and do some things differently  so we started thinking about bioreactors  we looked at a few different systems  there were three different systems that  we considered that were small footprint  single-use platforms one of them of  course was the through mobi CT quantum  cell expansion system one of the  platforms required the use of micro  carriers and the other two platforms the  cells grew out in a more traditional  two-dimensional model layer type of  manner however one of those platforms we  had to exclude kind of early on for  space considerations because although it  was indeed a small footprint type of  platform it still required a little bit  more instrumentation to go along with  the whole thing than we had space for so  in the end we narrowed it down to two  systems the micro carrier system in the  quantum and we brought them both  in-house and tested them for expanding  bone marrow MFCs so we thoroughly  evaluated these two systems and  eventually we decided that we had to  rule out the micro carrier system a big  part of this was the amount of  involvement in the time that we were  going to need to translate our current  2d system to a 3d system because really  if you've never worked in micro carriers  before they're just so different from  growing cells in flasks and there's a  lot of steps that need to be worked out  and developed along the way and we did a  lot of this during the evaluation phase  we did micro carrier screening assays  where we looked at a whole bunch of  different micro carriers and tried to  figure out which ones were going to work  best for our needs and for our cell type  there was also considerations regarding  the way that you have to prepare the  micro carrier some of them  our pre-sterilized some of them need to  be autoclaved some of them many of them  need to be pre hydrated and all of these  things you have to think about and in  fact we don't have easy access to an  autoclave we have to walk across the  street to get to one and so obviously  this wasn't really an ideal situation  for us regarding monitoring of the  culture you have to think about that not  knowing when the cells are going to be  confluent on the carriers and also you  need to keep a certain cell to micro  carrier ratio so after a certain amount  of time yes add more micro carriers and  you have to figure out how to how to  know when to do that so again just more  steps that need more development and  then there's the question of how to  harvest the cells from the micro  carriers and once you do how will you  separate the micro carriers from the  cells you may need additional equipment  like a fluidized bed centrifuge a lot of  the techniques micro carrier separation  techniques that were on the market at  the time when we did this evaluation  were designed for very large scale  applications and not really for  processing volumes in the 5 liter range  which is where we were and then after  all this how can you do your purity  testing how can you be sure that you  have no micro care contamination in the  final product so again these are all  things that we could have worked out  over time but each one of these steps  needed to be developed and tested and it  would have taken a very considerable  amount of time for the two technologists  that we had doing this work so again we  eventually had to rule this this  platform out so we narrowed our options  down to the platform but we still really  weren't sure if this was going to be  right for us there were some obvious  benefits to using the quantum like the  small footprint with the eliminative  space that we have and also the relative  ease of translating our current  protocols to using the bioreactor there  was a reduction in the amount of time it  took for us to expand the cells and also  we were able to after doing a number of  pilot runs with Guillermo's help get to  where we could produce greater cell  yields in the bioreactor versus using  flasks and we're also able to keep the  cells at a lower passage for banking and  for  production of MSC products but probably  the most valuable advantage for our  particular situation was the addition of  automation because it saved us so much  time with performing manual tasks and  there's also the possibility of  scalability with a quantum we have a  5,000 square-foot GMP facility that is  being planned out right now hopefully  for this year or next year and so  hopefully when we get this new facility  and we have a little bit more space we  can add more Quantum's  and once we're familiar with the  protocol we can just do the same  protocol with more Quantum's and able  and we'll be able to get more cell  yields so I'm trying to determine  whether or not this platform was going  to be right for us we had to ask the  question whether or not this automated  platform was going to measure up in  every sense to the current manual  protocol that we were using we looked at  a number of different parameters and we  did the analysis and we were able to put  out this poster that we presented the  world stem cell somewhat a couple years  ago if any was interested in looking at  this poster please feel free to email me  but I'm going to go over what we found  in the subsequent slides and again to  highlight work what our overarching  goals were first and foremost we needed  the ability to expand large quantities  of MSDS so that we could treat patients  in cell therapy and render medicine  clinical trials and manufacture those  cells in compliance with GMP standards  and so with this analysis we wanted to  be able to compare the automated versus  manual method with regard to cell yields  cost time and effort from the  technologist and just overall ease of  performing the protocols so what we did  was we started with 20 about 25 to 30  milliliters of fresh bone-marrow  aspirate and we performed the automated  method and the manual method basically  side by side we isolated and expanded  Romero MSC s using alpha mem and 5%  human play a little I say we created  master cell banks using the automated  method and the manual method and  products using both methods as well and  then we just quantified the difference  as we look  again cell yields we looked at the cost  of consumables and reagents needed to  perform these two different methods how  long it took to grow the cells how much  effort it required from the  technologists and how many technologists  we needed to do all the work first  looking at the automated method you're  able to dilute the bone marrow and add  it directly to the quantum system you  don't have to pre isolate the mono  nuclear cells as you normally do you do  have to pre coat the bioreactor with  fiber neck and overnight or else the  cells will not adhere in order to  monitor the culture you look at glucose  consumption lactate generation and you  can also see control flasks at the same  seating densities just to give you a  visual of how confluent the cells are to  help aid in your decision as to when the  cells are ready to be harvested you can  freeze down cells into vials as early as  p0 but you get a lot more cells at p1  and so we typically expand up to p1 to  create our master cell bank but you will  have additional vials at p0 that you can  further expand to p1 to create more  cells to Bank and then of course  eventually up to create more products  the product product bags can be  generated as early as passage 2 so you  pull vials from passage 1 from the  master cell Bank and then just expand  them up one more passage to create doses  and the total area surface area for  growth inside one bioreactor is 21,000  square centimeters for the manua