antum and the required passages  associated with using the quantum and  also the cost for expanding manually and  these are all the parameters that we  looked at we looked at how much did it  cost to run each passage that we would  need to run exactly how much time it  would take to actually expand each  passage how much hands-on time it would  take from the technologists actually  performing tasks and how many  technologists you would need to perform  the tasks and then of course we looked  at approximations of how many cells you  should be able to expect from each of  these passages doing it one way or the  other and how many  biles you can freeze down to create a  bank and how many doses you can expect  to manufacture and so finally we really  wanted to make sense of all of this data  and we wanted to kind of put it into  terms that would make sense to other  laboratories that were in the same  situation as a smaller laboratories that  want to support a clinical trial at  their institution that they have limited  resources and so what we did was we  imagined a hypothetical clinical trial  that happening at our institution where  each subject is supposed to receive one  dose of 100 million MSC s and we wanted  to ask whether or not it would be  possible for us to manufacture the doses  needed for this clinical trial and how  much would it cost us to do it so  looking at cost what we found was that  to manufacture these hundred doses using  the quantum they would cost about a  hundred thousand dollars or a thousand  dollars for those or $10 per million MSP  however doing it the manual way using  multi layer flasks in a single cell  culture incubator it would cost about  two hundred thousand dollars to create  the same number of doses and so the cost  were about double and the majority of  that cost came really from the cell  culture media and cell culture  supplement because doing the manual  method using multi-layer Flass that  require such large volumes of media 